Yes. The interesting synthesis is that “good” does not have to mean soft, passive, harmless, or sexually neutral.

A more attractive version of goodness is often strength under voluntary restraint:

\text{capacity} + \text{agency} + \text{self-control} + \text{care}

That is different from merely being agreeable.

A man can be assertive, ambitious, competitive, physically formidable, sexually confident, protective, decisive, and still be deeply prosocial. In fact, traits like courage, reliability, generosity, loyalty, competence, and protection become more meaningful when they are chosen by someone who also has the capacity to act otherwise.

So if you imagine sexual selection pushing toward “goodness,” the selector probably would not be:

\text{nicest person wins}

It would look more like:

\boxed{
\text{attraction}
=
f(
\text{competence},
\text{vitality},
\text{confidence},
\text{social intelligence},
\text{character},
\text{trustworthiness},
\text{desire}
)
}

And different people weight those terms differently.

There is also a signaling problem. Goodness that costs nothing is hard to distinguish from presentation. But someone who has power, options, or status and nevertheless behaves honorably provides a stronger signal:

\text{could exploit}
+
\text{chooses not to}
\rightarrow
\text{credible character information}

That may be closer to the selector you are describing: not replacing masculinity with harmlessness, but making integrated masculinity more desirable.

Predator/prey language even breaks down here. A mature pair bond is closer to:

\boxed{\text{two capable agents choosing mutual vulnerability}}

Both retain teeth. Neither needs to use them on the other.

And culturally, that is probably the lever: make admirable male characters good without making goodness their emasculation arc. Let the hero be dangerous when necessary, funny, sexual, ambitious, competent, protective—and have integrity because he chooses it.

Then goodness stops looking like the consolation prize for people who couldn’t dominate.

It becomes something a strong person is proud to be.